Here’s a refined overview of the Google Merchant XML product feed schema, along with an optimized example to help you set one up effectively:


---

Core Requirements & Schema Overview

1. Feed Format & Structure

Must follow RSS or Atom specs, use UTF-8 encoding, and use Google’s required XML namespaces (e.g. xmlns:g="http://base.google.com/ns/1.0").

Should be .xml (or .gz compressed) and ideally under 500 MB.



---

2. Required Item Tags

Every <item> should include:

<g:id> — Unique, stable across feeds, preferably your SKU.

<title> — Clear product name (no promotional fluff).

<g:description> — Accurate description, not overly promotional.

<link> — Direct landing page URL, no redirects.

<g:image_link> — High-quality image URL, at least 800×800 px for non-clothing.

<g:price> — Numeric + currency (e.g., 29.99 USD).

<g:availability> — One of: in stock, out of stock, or preorder.

<g:product_type> — Your own product category hierarchy.



---

3. Condition-Based Requirements

These depend on the product type or purpose:

brand — Required unless unbranded.

condition — Required; new, used, or refurbished.

For apparel or similar goods: color, size, gender, age_group – required.

shipping_weight — Required if shipping calculated by weight.



---

4. Optional Enhancements

Optional tags that can boost feed accuracy and visibility:

sale_price — Temporary discounted price.

gtin, mpn

additional_image_link

google_product_category

item_group_id — For variant grouping.

multipack, is_bundle – for grouped listings.



---

Optimized Example XML Feed

Here's a compact, well-structured example, including both mandatory and helpful optional tags:

<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rss version="2.0"
     xmlns:g="http://base.google.com/ns/1.0">
  <channel>
    <title>My Store Products</title>
    <link>https://www.example.com/</link>
    <description>Example product feed</description>

    <item>
      <g:id>SKU12345</g:id>
      <title>Men's Running Shoes – Black</title>
      <g:description>Lightweight running shoes with breathable mesh and durable rubber outsole.</g:description>
      <link>https://www.example.com/product12345</link>
      <g:image_link>https://www.example.com/images/product12345.jpg</g:image_link>
      <g:price>59.99 GBP</g:price>
      <g:availability>in stock</g:availability>
      <g:product_type>Footwear > Running Shoes</g:product_type>
      
      <!-- Optional but recommended -->
      <brand>BrandName</brand>
      <condition>new</condition>
      <g:google_product_category>Apparel & Accessories > Shoes</g:google_product_category>
      <g:gtin>1234567890123</g:gtin>
      <mpn>BN-RS-001</mpn>
      <g:color>Black</g:color>
      <g:size>10</g:size>
      <g:gender>male</g:gender>
      <g:shipping_weight>0.8 kg</g:shipping_weight>
      <g:sale_price>49.99 GBP</g:sale_price>
      <item_group_id>RUNSHOE123</item_group_id>
      <additional_image_link>https://www.example.com/images/product12345_side.jpg</additional_image_link>
    </item>

  </channel>
</rss>


---

Best Practices & Tips

Keep IDs stable across time, languages, and countries.

Ensure all URLs are direct, crawlable, and properly formatted.

Avoid promotional text in titles/descriptions—stick to objective, clear language.

For products like clothes with size variations, ensure each variant has its own item row (with unique id and size).

If using feed rules or supplemental feeds, you can enhance or override attributes like optimized titles without changing your main site.



